# The workspace hosts mutable application state and an upstream Harness copy.
# Git tracks only the private FreeCodeGo source beneath plugin/. Its nested
# .gitignore further limits that tree to FreeCodeGo-owned files.
/*
!/.gitignore
# The front page is a root-level file, so the blanket `/*` above drops it unless
# it is named here — measured on this repository's own first commit, which
# carried 816 of its 817 files because `git check-ignore -v README.md` reported
# `.gitignore:4:/*`. The same applies to anything added at the root later
# (a LICENSE, CI, or release output), which is the trade the blanket rule makes.
#
# The prediction came true twice in one pass: the front page gained a Chinese
# companion and a ``LICENSE`` so the repository's license is machine-readable
# from the root, and both would have been dropped silently without a name here.
!/README.md
!/README.zh.md
!/LICENSE
# The changelog the release takes its notes from: `.github/workflows/release-freecodego.yml`
# reads this file by path and refuses to publish a version whose section is
# missing. This is the third time this file's own prediction held — a root-level
# file the blanket `/*` above drops silently — and the first time the dropped
# file would have broken a release instead of just going unnoticed.
!/CHANGELOG.md
!/plugin/
!/plugin/**

# GitHub resolves workflows, issue and pull-request templates and Dependabot
# against `<repository root>/.github` only, so the copy that used to ship inside
# `plugin/` was published without ever being loaded — the release workflow
# included, and that workflow is what creates the release asset the community
# listing's entry pins. The metadata lives at the root now, and the blanket `/*`
# above is why it has to be named here.
!/.github/
!/.github/**

# The former nested repository is retained locally as a migration backup.
/plugin/.git/
/plugin/.git.plugin-repository-backup/

# A `.tmp-` name means throwaway, but the tree beneath plugin/ is tracked whole,
# so one-shot code-generation scripts get committed here by mistake and are
# cited nowhere. This file used to carry the rule that was meant to stop them,
# pointed at `harness-plugin/scripts/.tmp-*` — and it blocked nothing, because a
# deeper `.gitignore` overrides a shallower one: `plugin/.gitignore`'s
# `!/packages/freecodego/**` is what git reports as the deciding pattern for
# every such path, so three more scratch files were committed in the package
# root while this rule sat here naming them. The rule now lives beside that
# allowlist in `plugin/.gitignore`, where it is decided in favour of; a pattern
# kept here would only read as protection while providing none.
